  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 74426867712
    m_Localized: Temple History
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 488148819968
    m_Localized: Wat Khao Wong (Narai Cave) has been a <b>revered religious site</b>
      since the reign of <b>King Narai the Great (1656 - 1688)</b>, as King Narai
      passed through and <b>stayed at Narai Cave</b> on his journey to govern from
      Ayutthaya, leaving his residence at Narai Palace in Lopburi.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 880710508544
    m_Localized: "In the reign of <b>King Songtham (1610 - 1628)</b>, the king also
      visited to <b>worship the Buddha\u2019s footprint and stayed at this cave</b>,
      as the cave\u2019s climate remains comfortably cool year-round."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1036734423040
    m_Localized: "At Narai Cave, <b>ancient inscriptions</b> on the entrance wall
      reveal that it has been <b>a place of religious practice since the Anuradhapura
      period (over 1,200 years ago)</b>. Having experienced periods of prosperity
      and decline, the cave was revived as a Buddhist monastery <b>in line with Thailand\u2019s
      traditional governance and Buddhist heritage</b>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4689106805186560
    m_Localized: "Furthermore, historical records show that during the Sukhothai
      and Ayutthaya periods, <b>King Songtham sent a Thai delegation to Sri Lanka
      to worship the Buddha\u2019s footprint at Sumanakuta Hill</b>, and Sri Lankan
      monks shared that there was also a Buddha footprint at Thailand\u2019s Suwannabanphot
      Hill. <b>This confirms cultural exchanges in Buddhist traditions between the
      Lanka and Siam branches</b>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4688458831355904
    m_Localized: "At the entrance, <b>there are inscriptions in ancient \u2018Mon\u2019
      script (\u2018Pallava\u2019)</b>, used by South Indians. Evidence shows its
      presence in central and southern Thailand before the Sukhothai period, and
      <b>later, King Ramkhamhaeng the Great adapted this script, creating the foundation
      for Thai writing</b>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4688993881939968
    m_Localized: "Narai Cave\u2019s inscription, with <b>three lines of prose from
      the 7th century (\u2018Dvaravati\u2019 period)</b>, was translated by the Fine
      Arts Department:"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4689026685591552
    m_Localized: "<b>\u201CKantrajaya, founder of Anuradhapura, appointed Uncle Sinatha
      as his representative, along with the people of (Anuradhapura), to hold a celebratory
      dance for what is enshrined here.\u201D</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4689060504264704
    m_Localized: "This inscription suggests that <b>the local area was inhabited
      for centuries and might have been a prosperous settlement</b>. The name \u2018Anuradhapura\u2019
      reflects <b>ancient connections between the Lanka and \u2018Mon\u2019 peoples</b>,
      commemorated here in honor of shared heritage."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 5914118266544128
    m_Localized: "<b>This temple was granted permission for construction on July
      5, 1917, and received \u2018Visungkhama Seema\u2019 status on June 15, 1978</b>.
      The area covers 30 meters in width and 51 meters in length, totaling 1,500
      square meters. <b>The ordination boundary was consecrated on February 20, 1983,
      led by Somdet Phra Phutthacharn (Kiew Uppaseno) and Phra Thamma Sitthanaayok
      (Thongchai Sukhajano) of Wat Saket Rajaworamahavihara</b>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 5914122121109504
    m_Localized: Wat Khao Wong has been developed into <b>a model temple with outstanding
      achievements</b>, officially <b>registered as a historical site</b> by the
      Department of Fine Arts on December 4, 2002, as announced in the Royal Gazette,
      Volume 119, Special Issue 119 Ng.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 5914126562877440
    m_Localized: "Currently, Wat Khao Wong (Tham Narai) is located at 62/1, Moo 5,
      Khao Wong Subdistrict, Phra Phutthabat District, Saraburi Province. It <b>serves
      as a provincial meditation center in Saraburi</b>, practicing under <b>Phra
      Rajaprommayan (Luang Pho Ruesi Ling Dam)</b> with a focus on Maha Satipatthana
      Sutta meditation. The temple accepts meditation practitioners year-round, who
      must strictly adhere to the temple\u2019s regulations and traditions."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 5914133177294848
    m_Localized: "Additionally, Wat Khao Wong (Tham Narai) is a <b>habitat for rare
      flora and fauna</b>, such as Queen Moke (Wrightia sirikitiae D.J. Middleton
      & Santisuk), Chan Pha, Red-whiskered Bulbul, and albino squirrels, <b>preserved
      as part of Thailand\u2019s cultural heritage for future generations</b>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
